## Rust lesson

`FailureReason` is an enum, so the Rust side can `match` on every variant and the compiler checks that none is missed. The TypeScript side receives the code as a plain `string`, so the compiler cannot check it. That is why this bug went unnoticed: a wrong string literal compiles fine. The `default` branch in `statusForErrorCode` is a safe fallback, not a guarantee.[INFO] [KRS] <-- GenerateAssistantResponseCommand done totalEvents=256

